WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.111 --> 00:00:05.214
[MUSIC]

00:00:05.214 --> 00:00:10.410
The silence felt like it stretched to
infinity, and my faith was beet red,

00:00:10.410 --> 00:00:14.463
frozen like a deer in headlights,
I stammered for words.

00:00:14.463 --> 00:00:19.112
Finally I heard the voice of my general
partner coming and swooping to save me,

00:00:19.112 --> 00:00:22.916
like a remote and distant loudspeaker
from some far off planet,

00:00:22.916 --> 00:00:27.708
bringing me back to the present, to sand
hell road conference room to the perfect

00:00:27.708 --> 00:00:30.473
square of California
sunshine in front of me.

00:00:30.473 --> 00:00:34.045
Mina is not an executive assistant.

00:00:34.045 --> 00:00:36.616
She is an associate partner at this firm.

00:00:38.854 --> 00:00:41.291
A huge wave of relief washed over me and

00:00:41.291 --> 00:00:45.260
I let out the breath I didn't
even realize I had been holding.

00:00:46.600 --> 00:00:50.986
I tell you guys a story because it is
literally one of the most cringe worthy

00:00:50.986 --> 00:00:53.160
moments of my entire life.

00:00:53.160 --> 00:00:56.170
But also it because it is the moment,

00:00:56.170 --> 00:01:00.940
that I felt like an absolute
outsider in venture capital.

00:01:02.620 --> 00:01:06.944
I felt like an outsider because I was a 22
year old female in this old boys club of

00:01:06.944 --> 00:01:08.230
venture capital.

00:01:08.230 --> 00:01:10.728
And the truth was,
I probably was never going to fit in.

00:01:13.413 --> 00:01:17.985
Today, only 16% of venture
capital investors are women.

00:01:17.985 --> 00:01:26.910
And only 3% of all of venture capital
in America goes to female CEO's.

00:01:26.910 --> 00:01:29.482
This is crazy to me.

00:01:29.482 --> 00:01:35.152
My experiences as a recovering venture
capitalist and now as the founder of

00:01:35.152 --> 00:01:42.070
my own Y Combinator back startup are just
incredibly difficult for me to talk about.

00:01:42.070 --> 00:01:46.488
Because I have gone through all of
the stages of grief about how capital is

00:01:46.488 --> 00:01:49.080
allocated in our country.

00:01:49.080 --> 00:01:53.690
Denial, anger, just so
much anger and rage.

00:01:55.760 --> 00:02:01.157
How is it that this group of just
incredibly well meaning people who are my

00:02:01.157 --> 00:02:07.280
friends, who are my mentors could
yield systematically unequal outcomes.

00:02:07.280 --> 00:02:11.907
How is it that an industry that is
considered by most to be the most

00:02:11.907 --> 00:02:17.240
meritocratic in the world, has portfolio
that look the way that they do?

00:02:22.590 --> 00:02:27.804
And finally, I circled back to optimism,
optimism because all of the amazing

00:02:27.804 --> 00:02:32.544
female founders that I've met here
at Stanford, and all of the amazing

00:02:32.544 --> 00:02:37.620
female investors I know are going to go
on to change things in venture capital.

00:02:39.860 --> 00:02:44.396
I'm here to help you skip all of those
stages of grief that I went through and

00:02:44.396 --> 00:02:46.710
jumped to that optimism part as well.

00:02:48.710 --> 00:02:53.145
I want to start by just talking about some
of the biggest reasons I think exists for

00:02:53.145 --> 00:02:55.010
this persistent gap in funding.

00:02:56.290 --> 00:03:02.520
The first, is the pipeline problem or
probably not in the way that you think?

00:03:02.520 --> 00:03:06.730
Yes, women only get 20% of the computer
science degrees in this country.

00:03:09.320 --> 00:03:12.986
But the real problem is
that we are using that as

00:03:12.986 --> 00:03:16.283
a pipeline to figure out who gets funded.

00:03:16.283 --> 00:03:21.403
This outdated investment criteria
that technical chops are somehow

00:03:21.403 --> 00:03:26.000
a prerequisite for
being a startup CEO is absolute bullshit.

00:03:27.260 --> 00:03:30.354
We need to get rid of these archetypes
we have in our head of what

00:03:30.354 --> 00:03:32.960
an ideal startup founder looks like.

00:03:32.960 --> 00:03:37.334
Having moved to the operator side now,
I can tell you there is an enormous

00:03:37.334 --> 00:03:41.220
laundry list of things that you
need to be and a great CEO.

00:03:41.220 --> 00:03:44.840
Nowhere on that list is knowing how
to write a single line of code.

00:03:46.110 --> 00:03:50.307
And we need to make sure that women
are not opting themselves out because they

00:03:50.307 --> 00:03:52.351
don't check off all of these boxes.

00:03:58.848 --> 00:04:01.332
The second, which I know all too well,

00:04:01.332 --> 00:04:05.414
is the lack of diversity in
the decision makers in this country.

00:04:07.804 --> 00:04:11.611
It's funny how the people that you happen

00:04:11.611 --> 00:04:15.860
to trust just look a hell
of a lot like you.

00:04:15.860 --> 00:04:20.027
And all of those original
early stage venture capital

00:04:20.027 --> 00:04:22.440
decisions are built on trust.

00:04:24.210 --> 00:04:28.861
One thing I learned in venture capital is
that sourcing is often playing basketball

00:04:28.861 --> 00:04:31.199
with your Harvard fraternity brothers.

00:04:33.950 --> 00:04:38.760
The people that you trust just happen
to look a hell of a lot like you.

00:04:38.760 --> 00:04:40.890
Maybe they went to
the same schools as you.

00:04:40.890 --> 00:04:42.700
Maybe they were in the same fraternity.

00:04:44.510 --> 00:04:46.990
You have to check those types of things.

00:04:48.920 --> 00:04:50.839
The last is unconscious bias.

00:04:52.100 --> 00:04:57.144
In an HBS study published in 2017,
researchers found that when

00:04:57.144 --> 00:05:02.451
women walk in for a pitch, they are way
more likely to get questions that

00:05:02.451 --> 00:05:08.024
are about the potential risks of
the business versus the potential gains,

00:05:08.024 --> 00:05:11.765
even when giving identical
pitches as a male CEO.

00:05:14.409 --> 00:05:18.630
As a result, even when women
are given the opportunity to pitch,

00:05:18.630 --> 00:05:20.490
they often are overlooked.

00:05:21.750 --> 00:05:27.519
This is equivalent to asking one
set of Yelp reviewers in what way,

00:05:27.519 --> 00:05:34.019
was your food Michelin star and asked
me a different set of Yelp reviewers.

00:05:35.530 --> 00:05:37.570
In what way did your
servers screw up your meal?

00:05:40.470 --> 00:05:43.500
The real world is obviously
a lot more subtle than that.

00:05:44.840 --> 00:05:48.645
But all of us have these unconscious bias
sees that impact the way that we make

00:05:48.645 --> 00:05:49.960
decisions in this world.

00:05:53.420 --> 00:06:01.330
I wanted to take a moment to just reflect
on, Why this matters so much in the world.

00:06:04.077 --> 00:06:08.588
Venture capital is a tiny industry,
but it is an important gatekeeper

00:06:08.588 --> 00:06:12.189
to the types of ideas that end
up existing in this world.

00:06:14.244 --> 00:06:18.879
What kind of amazing ideas are we missing
out on because the group of people

00:06:18.879 --> 00:06:23.010
we are backing come from such
a small homogenous and elite group.

00:06:24.930 --> 00:06:31.387
Second, equity returns are a huge driver
of wealth creation in this country.

00:06:31.387 --> 00:06:36.570
50% of all fortune five companies
originally VC or Angel Bact.

00:06:38.220 --> 00:06:43.006
So if you look at all of those inequities,
all of the systematic wealth

00:06:43.006 --> 00:06:47.956
inequities in this country, a lot of
those originate at an earlier stage

00:06:47.956 --> 00:06:52.905
than you would think, at how capital
is allocated In the beginning, and

00:06:52.905 --> 00:06:57.399
to whom capital gains accrue both
on the founder and funder side.

00:07:00.813 --> 00:07:06.020
What I said today for women holds true for
so many other groups as well.

00:07:07.130 --> 00:07:13.480
These marginalized communities
are also in need of allies Lastly,

00:07:13.480 --> 00:07:18.082
y'all are just missing out on
some crazy outsized returns.

00:07:18.082 --> 00:07:23.350
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Companies

00:07:23.350 --> 00:07:27.510
founded by women are faster
to reach profitability.

00:07:27.510 --> 00:07:29.776
They're more capital efficient,

00:07:29.776 --> 00:07:33.710
they literally returned twice
as much per capital invested.

00:07:36.850 --> 00:07:41.860
As with most asset classes it
pays to invest in the underdog.

00:07:45.250 --> 00:07:50.437
This past year marked a record in
flow of capital into renter capital.

00:07:50.437 --> 00:07:55.340
And yet, over that same time period,
the percentage of women

00:07:55.340 --> 00:07:59.690
getting allocated that
capital actually decreased.

00:07:59.690 --> 00:08:04.425
It pisses me off in so
many ways to see the numbers slide

00:08:04.425 --> 00:08:09.913
backwards from 3% to something
even lower [LAUGH] than 3%.

00:08:12.081 --> 00:08:15.550
Yet there's also so
much to be optimistic about.

00:08:15.550 --> 00:08:19.810
Last year also marked one of the biggest
IPOs ever by a female founder and

00:08:19.810 --> 00:08:21.290
Stitch Fix.

00:08:21.290 --> 00:08:25.949
And one of the largest funding rounds ever
in women's health, which is just been

00:08:25.949 --> 00:08:30.349
an area that has been so systematically
under invested in for generations.

00:08:32.480 --> 00:08:35.629
We still have so
far to go to get to 50% but

00:08:35.629 --> 00:08:39.580
we have so much to be proud of and
to celebrate as well.

00:08:42.120 --> 00:08:45.960
I am tired and sick of waiting on
the sidelines for the numbers to change.

00:08:47.000 --> 00:08:49.920
I am in a room of movers and shakers.

00:08:49.920 --> 00:08:53.950
And I know that if we commit to this,
we can change it.

00:08:55.690 --> 00:08:59.637
I'd love to re center again on
the things that really matter and

00:08:59.637 --> 00:09:01.280
the things that we can do.

00:09:02.730 --> 00:09:06.646
To the women in this room,
for our founders,

00:09:06.646 --> 00:09:10.781
you inspire me to be
a better version of myself.

00:09:13.216 --> 00:09:15.850
Thank you and I'm with you.

00:09:15.850 --> 00:09:18.420
I will be your hype woman for life.

00:09:20.299 --> 00:09:25.392
To the women who are considering
entrepreneurship but holding themselves

00:09:25.392 --> 00:09:30.420
back for whatever reason, I believe
in you and you should own that space.

00:09:32.602 --> 00:09:34.068
To the Allies in the room?

00:09:36.843 --> 00:09:41.284
We need you to believe in us,
to mentor us to invest in us.

00:09:43.967 --> 00:09:46.120
And lastly, to the investors in the room.

00:09:48.420 --> 00:09:50.878
Change the face of your industry,

00:09:50.878 --> 00:09:56.150
it is not enough to just hire
a token female junior team member.

00:09:56.150 --> 00:10:00.940
It is exhausting to be [LAUGH]
the token junior female team member.

00:10:02.460 --> 00:10:04.015
The solution is to hire at least two.

00:10:04.015 --> 00:10:11.867
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; But

00:10:11.867 --> 00:10:15.128
recognize that you will
need to mentor them.

00:10:15.128 --> 00:10:20.104
They will face just immeasurable
uphill challenges as torchbearers

00:10:20.104 --> 00:10:22.772
in the industry of venture capital.

00:10:25.621 --> 00:10:30.754
And lastly, I find it fun people who
don't look like you, because it literally

00:10:30.754 --> 00:10:35.520
makes financial sense to do so but
also because it is the right thing to do.

00:10:37.450 --> 00:10:40.840
I know that the next Mark Zuckerberg
will not look like him.

00:10:43.170 --> 00:10:45.940
That founder is probably in this room.

00:10:45.940 --> 00:10:49.588
Go be her go work for her or go fund her,

00:10:49.588 --> 00:10:54.288
because I know she will
return your entire fund.

00:10:54.288 --> 00:10:56.303
Thank you.

00:10:56.303 --> 00:10:57.792
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:10:57.792 --> 00:11:02.618
[MUSIC]

